# 16. The short version of the architecture

```text
Beads = task graph
Git Nexus = repo graph
PAL = agent/team planner
MCP = tool/context layer
A2A = agent/task transport
Claude Code = deep implementation runtime
Codex = review/test/patch runtime
Recoder Code = native runtime
Playwright MCP = browser verifier
Daytona/E2B = sandbox
Context7/mem0/pgvector = context + memory
Artifacts = evidence ledger
Recoder = canonical state, policy, compiler, approvals, verification, UI
```

This is the clean path:

```text
Do not add more agents first.
First make every existing project speak TaskSpec, InstructionBundle, RunEvent, Artifact, Approval, and MemoryCandidate.
```

Once that exists, every OSS piece you already have becomes useful instead of becoming more complexity.

[1]: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory "How Claude remembers your project - Claude Code Docs"
[2]: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills "Extend Claude with skills - Claude Code Docs"
[3]: https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md "Custom instructions with AGENTS.md – Codex | OpenAI Developers"
[4]: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18 "Specification - Model Context Protocol"
[5]: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/ "Overview - A2A Protocol"
[6]: https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks "Hooks – Codex | OpenAI Developers"
You’re right. I under-counted your stack.

The better framing is:

```text
Recoder already has many OSS / forked / vendored / internal platform pieces.
Do not pick only 3–4.
Create an OSS Component Registry and make every piece speak the same Agent OS contracts.
```

I confirmed the GitHub connector sees `caelum0x/recoder.xyz` as the large private main repo on `main`, with `recoder-code` and `recoder-web` also installed as private repos.

---

# Correct Recoder direction

Recoder should not be:

```text
Claude wrapper
Codex wrapper
MCP directory
prompt library
one more workflow builder
one more agent framework
```

Recoder should be:

```text
All-OSS Agent OS for software work
```

Where:

```text
Beads        = task graph
Git Nexus    = repo/code intelligence
PAL          = planner / agent-team router
MCP          = tool/context protocol
A2A          = agent/task protocol
Claude Code  = deep coding runtime
Codex        = review/test/patch runtime
Recoder Code = native Recoder runtime
Playwright   = browser verification
Daytona/E2B  = sandboxes
OpenClaw     = vendored agent capability source
skills       = reusable no-manual-prompt workflows
artifacts    = evidence ledger
PM/Agency    = approval + delivery layer
monitoring   = production truth
infra         = self-host runtime layer
```

Anthropic’s docs support this architecture because Claude Code is designed around persistent `CLAUDE.md`, auto memory, rules, imports, and skills, not repeated manual explanation. Claude’s docs also say `CLAUDE.md` and memory are context rather than enforcement, so Recoder still needs hooks, policy, approvals, and server-side controls. ([Claude API Docs][1]) Codex similarly uses `AGENTS.md` instruction files and deterministic hooks for lifecycle behavior, validation, logging, and policy guardrails. ([OpenAI Developers][2])

---

# 1. The missing layer: Recoder OSS Component Registry

Before building more features, add a registry that lists **every Recoder OSS/internal/forked component** and what contract it satisfies.

Create:

```text
oss/
├── manifests/
│   ├── recoder-components.yaml
│   ├── oss-components.yaml
│   ├── licenses.yaml
│   ├── patch-policy.yaml
│   └── component-owners.yaml
├── patches/
│   ├── openclaw/
│   ├── opencode/
│   ├── qwen-code/
│   ├── gemini-cli/
│   ├── playwright-mcp/
│   ├── daytona/
│   ├── mcp/
│   └── a2a/
└── recipes/
    ├── docker-compose.full-oss.yml
    ├── docker-compose.agent-os.yml
    ├── docker-compose.observability.yml
    ├── docker-compose.sandbox.yml
    └── docker-compose.local-agents.yml
```

Each component gets a descriptor:

```ts
type RecoderComponent = {
  id: string;
  name: string;

  origin:
    | "recoder-native"
    | "vendored-oss"
    | "forked-oss"
    | "external-oss"
    | "protocol"
    | "runtime"
    | "infrastructure";

  location: {
    repo?: string;
    path?: string;
    packageName?: string;
    dockerImage?: string;
    externalUrl?: string;
  };

  contract:
    | "TaskGraph"
    | "RepoIntelligence"
    | "Planner"
    | "InstructionSource"
    | "InstructionCompiler"
    | "RuntimeAdapter"
    | "McpServer"
    | "A2AAgent"
    | "SandboxProvider"
    | "BrowserVerifier"
    | "MemoryProvider"
    | "ArtifactStore"
    | "EventBus"
    | "PolicyEngine"
    | "ObservabilityBackend"
    | "DeploymentProvider"
    | "SupportInbox"
    | "UiSurface";

  healthCheck: {
    command?: string;
    endpoint?: string;
    expected?: string;
  };

  security: {
    requiresAuth: boolean;
    handlesSecrets: boolean;
    canExecuteCode: boolean;
    canTouchProduction: boolean;
  };

  status:
    | "active"
    | "experimental"
    | "vendored"
    | "needs-contract"
    | "deprecated"
    | "retired";
};
```

Then Recoder can answer:

```text
What OSS do we have?
Where is it?
What contract does it implement?
Can it execute code?
Can it access secrets?
Can it write files?
Can it deploy?
Is it healthy?
Who owns it?
What tests prove it works?
```

