# 9. Full launch loop

Launch this, not “everything everywhere”:

```text
1. Import Beads issue.
2. Git Nexus creates ContextPack.
3. PAL creates agent plan.
4. Recoder compiles InstructionBundle.
5. Claude Code implements.
6. Codex reviews/tests.
7. Playwright MCP verifies preview.
8. Daytona/E2B provides sandbox if needed.
9. Artifacts are attached.
10. Human approves risky edge.
11. Beads/GitHub/PM updates.
12. Memory candidate is proposed.
13. Monitoring shows health.
```

This proves every major OSS piece works together.

---

# 10. The exact first task spec

```yaml
title: "Recoder Agent OS: register all OSS/internal components and wire prompt compiler foundation"
intent: platform_hardening
repo: caelum0x/recoder.xyz

goal: >
  Create the all-OSS Agent OS foundation. Recoder must stop treating Beads,
  Git Nexus, PAL, MCP, A2A, Claude Code, Codex, Recoder Code, Playwright MCP,
  Daytona, OpenClaw-derived modules, skills, artifacts, monitoring, infra, and
  PM/Agency surfaces as separate islands. Every component must register what
  contract it implements, and the instruction compiler must be able to consume
  TaskSpec + ContextPack + PALPlan to produce runtime-specific InstructionBundles.

known_visible_components:
  instruction:
    - AGENTS.md
    - .claude
    - .mcp.json
    - .recoderhints
    - skills
    - recoder-skill
  task_and_artifacts:
    - .beads
    - .artifacts
    - .omx/plans
    - tasks
    - issues/slack-for-agents
  apps:
    - recoder-web
    - recoder-agents
    - recoder-pm
    - recoder-agency
    - apps
    - app
    - components
  runtime:
    - homebrew-recoder
    - recoder-code
    - docker-backend
    - mcp
    - extracted-from-openclaw
    - daytona-projects
    - .playwright-mcp
  shared_and_code:
    - packages
    - shared
    - internal-packages
    - lib
    - backend
    - config
    - tools
    - scripts
    - tests
    - turbo/generators
  infra:
    - infra
    - nginx
    - monitoring
    - .github/workflows
    - .husky
    - .devcontainer
    - .env.*.example
    - .env.*.template
  release:
    - homebrew-recoder
    - .expo
    - .env.desktop-release.example

deliverables:
  - oss/manifests/recoder-components.yaml
  - oss/manifests/oss-components.yaml
  - oss/manifests/component-owners.yaml
  - packages/oss-registry/src/descriptor.ts
  - packages/oss-registry/src/registry.ts
  - packages/agent-schemas/src/task-spec.schema.ts
  - packages/agent-schemas/src/context-pack.schema.ts
  - packages/agent-schemas/src/pal-plan.schema.ts
  - packages/agent-schemas/src/instruction-bundle.schema.ts
  - packages/agent-instructions/src/compiler.ts
  - packages/agent-instructions/src/renderers/claude-code-renderer.ts
  - packages/agent-instructions/src/renderers/codex-renderer.ts
  - docs/agent-os/01-all-oss-inventory.md
  - docs/agent-os/02-contracts.md
  - docs/agent-os/03-prompt-itself.md
  - scripts/oss-stack-health.ts
  - scripts/check-agent-instructions.ts

constraints:
  - no production behavior change in first PR
  - no hardcoded secrets or production URLs
  - do not rewrite Claude/Codex adapters yet
  - every component must declare contract, path, health check, status, and security risk
  - compiler MVP may use placeholder loaders for Beads/Git Nexus/PAL if needed
  - InstructionBundle must include provenance and verification commands

verification:
  - pnpm format
  - pnpm check-types
  - pnpm test
  - pnpm oss:health
  - pnpm agent:instructions:check

acceptance:
  - All visible Recoder components are listed in recoder-components.yaml.
  - Each component maps to a Recoder contract.
  - A sample TaskSpec compiles into Claude and Codex InstructionBundles.
  - InstructionBundle includes source refs for AGENTS.md, Beads, Git Nexus, PAL, policy, tools, and verification.
  - Docs explain how Recoder uses all OSS pieces as one Agent OS.
```

---

# Final correction

The architecture should not say:

```text
Use Beads, Git Nexus, PAL, MCP.
```

It should say:

```text
Everything in Recoder becomes a component in the Agent OS.

Beads, Git Nexus, PAL, MCP, A2A, Claude Code, Codex, Recoder Code,
Playwright MCP, Daytona, E2B, OpenClaw, Qwen/Gemini/OpenCode runtimes,
skills, artifacts, monitoring, infra, Slack/Chatwoot, PM, Agency, Web,
packages, shared, scripts, tests, and deploy surfaces all speak the same
contracts.
```

The contracts are the system:

```text
TaskSpec
ContextPack
PALPlan
InstructionBundle
RuntimeAdapter
McpToolCall
A2ATaskEnvelope
RunEvent
Artifact
ApprovalRequest
MemoryCandidate
WorkLock
PolicyDecision
OSSComponentDescriptor
```

Once those exist, Recoder can use **all** your OSS without becoming chaos.

[1]: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory "How Claude remembers your project - Claude Code Docs"
[2]: https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md "Custom instructions with AGENTS.md – Codex | OpenAI Developers"
[3]: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18 "Specification - Model Context Protocol"
[4]: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/ "Overview - A2A Protocol"
